{"cuisine/sudanese-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/sudanese-cuisine","n":"Sudanese cuisine","c":"#e46a3f","crumb":[["Africa","cuisines#africa"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Sudan","intro":[["Sudanese cuisine is greatly affected by the historical cross-cultural influences of Arab, Nubian, Egyptian, Turkish, Greek, and Levantine cuisine in Sudan. Many Sudanese foods have been around for thousands of years. The most common meats eaten are lamb and beef, in accordance with the Islamic halal laws. Most meals are communal and often shared with family, neighbors, and guests, as part of Sudanese hospitality."],["Breads such as baladi or aish baladi, a popular pita bread and gorrassa—a pancake-like bread similar to a crêpe—are eaten with savory stews (mullah), cheese (jibna), fava beans, and falafel (tamiya). There is also kisra, fermented bread similar to Ethiopian injera, but thinner and smaller. Khubz Francesa or Sudanese style French bread."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"sudanese-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sudanese_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"The Sudanese Kitchen","ref":"site/sudanese-kitchen","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"sudanese.kitchen","note":"Sudanese home cooking recipes and food history collected by Omer Al Tijani (Eltigani).","url":"https://www.sudanese.kitchen/about","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"The Sudanese Kitchen","ref":"cookbook/the-sudanese-kitchen","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Omer Al Tijani","note":"Cookbook mapping Sudan's regional food traditions, published by the Almas Art Foundation.","url":"https://sudanese.kitchen/cookbook","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Cabbage","ingredient/cabbage"],["Ground meat","ingredient/ground-meat"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."}],"cont":"African cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/4/47/Tepeln%C3%A1_%C3%BAprava_j%C3%ADdla_%28S%C3%BAd%C3%A1n%29_001.jpg/500px-Tepeln%C3%A1_%C3%BAprava_j%C3%ADdla_%28S%C3%BAd%C3%A1n%29_001.jpg","w":500,"h":667,"by":"Dominik HES","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0 cz","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/cz/deed.en","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tepeln%C3%A1_%C3%BAprava_j%C3%ADdla_(S%C3%BAd%C3%A1n)_001.jpg"}},"cuisine/fast-food":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/fast-food","n":"Fast food","c":"#a99fb5","crumb":[["Cuisine"]],"sub":"Style of cooking","intro":[["Fast food is a type of mass-produced food designed for commercial resale, with a strong priority placed on speed of service. Fast food is a commercial term, limited to food sold in a restaurant or store with frozen, preheated or precooked ingredients and served in packaging for take-out or takeaway. Fast food was created as a commercial strategy to accommodate large numbers of busy commuters, travelers and wage workers. In 2018, the fast-food industry was worth an estimated $570 billion globally."],["The fastest form of fast food consists of pre-cooked meals which reduce waiting periods to mere seconds. Other fast-food outlets, primarily hamburger outlets such as McDonald's and Burger King, use mass-produced, pre-prepared ingredients and cook the meat and french fries fresh, before assembling to order."],["Fast-food restaurants are traditionally distinguished by the drive-through. Outlets may be stands or kiosks, which may provide no shelter or seating, or fast-food restaurants (also known as quick-service restaurants). Franchise operations that are part of restaurant chains have standardized foodstuffs shipped to each restaurant from central locations."],["Many fast foods tend to be high in saturated fat, sugar, salt and calories. Fast-food consumption has been linked to increased risk of cardiovascular disease, colorectal cancer, obesity, high cholesterol, insulin resistance conditions and depression. These correlations remain strong even when controlling for confounding lifestyle variables, suggesting a strong association between fast-food consumption and increased risk of disease and early mortality."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fast_food"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]},{"h":"Dishes","count":2,"links":[["Burger King (Mattoon, Illinois)","dish/burger-king-mattoon-illinois"],["The Varsity (restaurant)","dish/the-varsity-restaurant"]]}],"cont":null,"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/6/68/In-N-Out_Burger_cheeseburgers.jpg/500px-In-N-Out_Burger_cheeseburgers.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Jef Poskanzer from Berkeley, California, USA","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:In-N-Out_Burger_cheeseburgers.jpg"}},"ingredient/mung-bean-sprout":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/mung-bean-sprout","n":"Mung bean sprout","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 12 dishes across 4 cuisines","intro":[["Mung bean sprouts are a culinary vegetable grown by sprouting mung beans. They can be grown by placing and watering the sprouted beans in the shade until the hypocotyls grow long. Mung bean sprouts are extensively cultivated and consumed in East and Southeast Asia and are very easy to grow, requiring minimal care other than a steady supply of water."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-mung-bean-sprout"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mung_bean_sprout"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":4,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Bakwan","dish/bakwan"],["Karedok","dish/karedok"],["Ketoprak (dish)","dish/ketoprak-dish"],["Lotek (food)","dish/lotek-food"],["Mie aceh","dish/mie-aceh"],["Mie celor","dish/mie-celor"],["